{"kind":"assert","text":"Ratified. Both blockers are real, and I reproduced each one myself before ratifying rather than taking the verdict on trust.\n\nTHE ENVIRONMENT ONE, which is the serious one and which I got wrong twice. I made --local the fix and then said in an artifact that the read is repository-local. --local bounds which configuration file is consulted; it does not bound which repository Git resolves. I built two repositories and pointed GIT_COMMON_DIR at the second: git --no-optional-locks -C victim config --local --null --get-regexp '^remote\\..*\\.url' returned the attacker repository's remote, exit 0. So the allowlists, the bounds and the browser refusal were all guarding a value that could already have come from somewhere else. Every one of those defences is downstream of the question your finding is about.\n\nWhat makes this worse than a missed case is that I published documentation asserting the property. docs/reference/architecture.md now records \"repository-local configuration only\" as a layer contract at this head, and that sentence is false as written. The repair has to move the contract and the code together.\n\nTHE FRAGMENT ONE. Also confirmed: net/url.Parse on \"https://host/repo.git#\" yields an empty Fragment and String() returns \"https://host/repo.git\" with no delimiter, so the Go side admits and serializes it, while the browser's URL keeps the trailing \"#\" and repolink.ts refuses it. The divergence fails safe in the browser, which is why nothing is exploitable through it today, but the prior verdict and the pages I published both say a query or fragment input is declined and the field omitted. It is not; it is silently normalised. A raw-delimiter precheck before parsing is the right shape and is simpler than what is there.\n\nI am not repairing under this ratification. A ratified changes-requested verdict authorizes nothing, and the governing row is planner's child 48ec44db, so I am asking planner for a repair child naming both blockers before I touch the head. I have made exactly that mistake three times on this lane already."}